00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:31.179
Propelan at Starbase is the engineering deep dive today, because a reusable stack of this

00:04:31.179 --> 00:04:38.119
size cannot live on tanker trucks. One stacked super heavy and starship holds about 5,250 tons

00:04:38.119 --> 00:04:46.519
of methalox, liquid methane and liquid oxygen, split as 4,100 tons of oxygen and 1,150 tons

00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:53.399
of methane at a 3.6 to 1 oxidizer to fuel ratio. Scaling to the annual cadence now under review,

00:04:53.579 --> 00:04:59.299
therefore asks for about 2.4 million tons of propellant a year. On a back of envelope split,

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:02.959
But that is on the order of 6500 tons a day, every day.

00:05:03.559 --> 00:05:08.139
Halling that mass from distant plants would hit a logistics wall because each tanker

00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:10.119
delivers only tens of tons per trip.

00:05:10.739 --> 00:05:15.079
If a load is on the order of 20 tons, you are looking at hundreds of cryogenic trips

00:05:15.079 --> 00:05:15.479
a day.

00:05:16.019 --> 00:05:17.219
A queue, not a pad.

00:05:17.899 --> 00:05:23.379
SpaceX is instead putting air separation units at starbase, plants that distill oxygen out

00:05:23.379 --> 00:05:29.219
of ambient air, which is about 21% oxygen by volume, and running a natural gas pipe

00:05:29.219 --> 00:05:34.159
pipeline called starpipe to on-site liquefaction that turns methane feed stock into liquid.

00:05:34.679 --> 00:05:40.279
In principle, the same cold box also yields nitrogen, and starbase already uses large amounts

00:05:40.279 --> 00:05:45.099
of liquid nitrogen to chill and purge the loading lines, so one industrial plant can

00:05:45.099 --> 00:05:46.719
feed two cryogenic problems.

00:05:47.339 --> 00:05:50.639
That layout collapses the idiot index of delivered propellant.

00:05:51.019 --> 00:05:54.319
How many times the finished commodity costs over its raw inputs?

00:05:54.319 --> 00:05:58.939
by cutting repeated liquefaction, road transport, and re-chill that otherwise

00:05:58.939 --> 00:06:00.319
stacked cost and energy.

00:06:00.899 --> 00:06:05.219
Once the local plants are in steady state, the marginal cost per tonne should approach

00:06:05.219 --> 00:06:11.039
the magic wand number, raw electricity and natural gas prices, not the convoy markup.

00:06:11.479 --> 00:06:16.639
A trucking path at several times those inputs is a fair estimate, and highway boil-off only

00:06:16.639 --> 00:06:17.459
widens the gap.

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:23.019
Star Factory is built to roll out one starship every day at Starbase, and the propellant

00:06:23.019 --> 00:06:27.939
system has to match that manufacturing pace. The building sits on the Gulf Coast in South

00:06:27.939 --> 00:06:33.239
Texas, with the pads a couple of miles to the east. Space X is trying to leave the old

00:06:33.239 --> 00:06:38.519
pattern in which tanker trucks fed pads in Florida and California. A method it also used

00:06:38.519 --> 00:06:43.359
for the first several years at Starbase. A reusable starship dwarfs those rockets,

00:06:43.939 --> 00:06:49.299
more than 400 feet tall, with room for more than a million gallons of super cold methane and oxygen.

00:06:49.299 --> 00:06:54.419
engine. That propellant load belongs to the whole Earth launch stack, not to a ship sitting

00:06:54.419 --> 00:07:00.479
on Mars. Super heavy, a 72 meter first stage does the first part of the climb and separates.

00:07:01.079 --> 00:07:06.319
The 52 meter upper starship continues alone. If that ship is ever to land on Mars and

00:07:06.319 --> 00:07:12.239
leave again, it needs a fresh supply of fuel and oxidizer. Methane makes that return conceivable,

00:07:12.639 --> 00:07:17.599
because Mars appears to offer the carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen required to make both liquids.

